(version 1)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LUM-CAR-A hand-written design rules
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS (TRAP-1, grep-verified at P2):
#   rules_gen.py NEVER reads constraints.json's `voltages` key. The net class it
#   emits for power nets carries only max(fab_min, 0.2) mm clearance, so NOTHING
#   makes the P7 router honour the 48 V clearance requirement - the violation
#   would only surface at P8 check_creepage, after routing is done.
#
#   Keyed on A.NetName, NOT A.Net: A.Net silently matches nothing
#   (LEARNINGS 2026-07-22). A LATER rule wins, so order matters.
#
# THE NUMBER: 0.635 mm, not 0.60 mm.
#   IPC-2221B B2 (external, uncoated, 51-100 V) gives 0.60 mm for a 57 V worst
#   case. The TPS2378 datasheet's own layout section asks for 0.025 in =
#   0.635 mm between VSS and high-voltage signals such as VDD. Rather than waive
#   a vendor recommendation on the board's most safety-critical IC, the LARGER
#   number governs board-wide. ICD-01 rev A2+ s5.1 is the authority.
#
#   The B4 "permanent polymer coating" column (0.13 mm) is deliberately NOT
#   claimed: standard LPI soldermask is not a qualified conformal coating, and
#   check_creepage implements only the uncoated columns, so a layout designed to
#   0.13 mm fails P8 with no waiver mechanism.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# SCOPE CORRECTION (P6): the first draft of this rule had no pad-pair exclusion
# and fired 30 times between ADJACENT PINS OF THE SAME PACKAGE - U1 (SOIC-8,
# 0.20 mm pitch gap), U22 (HTSSOP-20, 0.25 mm) and across C61/C62/C63's own two
# pads (0.59 mm). No placement can fix a 0.65 mm-pitch package, and that is not
# what the rule is for.
#
# TI's 0.635 mm guidance governs BOARD copper around the part - traces, vias,
# pours - not the vendor's own lead frame. Intra-package pin spacing is a
# package property, and this board verified every land against its vendor
# drawing at P3 (fp_verify + hand checks on J3/J4/J1).
#
# Pad-to-pad is excluded here and NOT lost: P8 check_creepage independently
# models pad-to-pad creepage from constraints.json `voltages`, so inter-
# footprint pad spacing is still gated - just by the checker that understands
# it rather than by a routing rule.

# P7 SCOPE AMENDMENT - the P6 pad-pair exclusion, carried one step further.
#
# The pad-pair exclusion stops the rule firing BETWEEN two pins of one package,
# but not between a pin and the FAN-OUT STUB that has to land on that pin.
# Measured on the placed board (work/p7/hv_escape.py): 15 of the 37 48 V pads
# sit closer than 0.635 mm to foreign copper OF THEIR OWN FOOTPRINT - U1 pads
# 1/9 (SOIC-8, 0.200 mm), U22 pads 1/2/3/6/18/19/20 (HTSSOP-20, 0.250-0.375 mm
# to pins 4/5/17 and to the pin-21 thermal land), U20 pad 3 (0.295 mm to its own
# GND exposed pad) and C1/C61/C62/C63 (0.590 mm across their own two 0805 pads).
# A track reaching any of those pads inherits the pad's neighbourhood, so under
# the un-amended rule those 15 pads were UNROUTABLE. Verified live at P7
# (work/p7/hvtest): a V48_RAW stub on U22 pad 6 fired HV_48V_clearance twice at
# 0.250 mm against pins 5 and 7 - a spacing the package pitch fixes and that no
# routing choice can change.
#
# The exclusion below applies the pad-pair rationale to the stub: when BOTH
# items lie inside ONE named footprint's courtyard, the spacing is package
# geometry. It is enumerated per refdes (never wildcarded) so it cannot quietly
# widen, and it covers only the seven footprints measured above. Every trace,
# via and pour in open board area - and any pair spanning two different
# footprints - still holds 0.635 mm. P8 check_creepage still models pad-to-pad
# independently from constraints.json `voltages`.
# Pre-amendment copy: work/p7/lumina-carrier.kicad_dru.pre_p7
(rule "HV_48V_clearance"
	(constraint clearance (min 0.635mm))
	(condition "(A.NetName == '+48V_SW' || A.NetName == 'V48_RAW' || A.NetName == 'V48_RTN') && !(A.Type == 'Pad' && B.Type == 'Pad') && !(A.intersectsCourtyard('U1') && B.intersectsCourtyard('U1')) && !(A.intersectsCourtyard('U20') && B.intersectsCourtyard('U20')) && !(A.intersectsCourtyard('U22') && B.intersectsCourtyard('U22')) && !(A.intersectsCourtyard('C1') && B.intersectsCourtyard('C1')) && !(A.intersectsCourtyard('C61') && B.intersectsCourtyard('C61')) && !(A.intersectsCourtyard('C62') && B.intersectsCourtyard('C62')) && !(A.intersectsCourtyard('C63') && B.intersectsCourtyard('C63'))")
	(severity error))

# The three 48 V nets must also hold 0.635 mm against EACH OTHER, not just
# against everything else. V48_RAW to V48_RTN is the full 57 V across the
# rectified rail; +48V_SW to V48_RTN is the same once the eFuse is on.
# Same pad-pair exclusion as above, for the same reason. Without it these two
# rules fire on U1's own adjacent VDD/VSS pins (SOIC-8, 0.200 mm) and across
# C1's own two pads (0.590 mm) - C1 being the 100 nF/100 V VDD-VSS bypass that
# is REQUIRED to sit right there by the PD detection-signature window.
# Those are package geometry, not routing choices. P8 check_creepage still
# models pad-to-pad from `voltages`.
# P7: these two carry the SAME same-courtyard amendment as HV_48V_clearance -
# without it a stub landing on U1 pad 1 (V48_RAW) fires against U1 pad 9
# (V48_RTN) at 0.200 mm, and C1's two pads do the same at 0.590 mm.
(rule "HV_48V_to_HV_48V"
	(constraint clearance (min 0.635mm))
	(condition "A.NetName == '+48V_SW' && B.NetName == 'V48_RTN' && !(A.Type == 'Pad' && B.Type == 'Pad') && !(A.intersectsCourtyard('U1') && B.intersectsCourtyard('U1')) && !(A.intersectsCourtyard('U20') && B.intersectsCourtyard('U20')) && !(A.intersectsCourtyard('U22') && B.intersectsCourtyard('U22')) && !(A.intersectsCourtyard('C1') && B.intersectsCourtyard('C1')) && !(A.intersectsCourtyard('C61') && B.intersectsCourtyard('C61')) && !(A.intersectsCourtyard('C62') && B.intersectsCourtyard('C62')) && !(A.intersectsCourtyard('C63') && B.intersectsCourtyard('C63'))")
	(severity error))

(rule "HV_48V_raw_to_rtn"
	(constraint clearance (min 0.635mm))
	(condition "A.NetName == 'V48_RAW' && B.NetName == 'V48_RTN' && !(A.Type == 'Pad' && B.Type == 'Pad') && !(A.intersectsCourtyard('U1') && B.intersectsCourtyard('U1')) && !(A.intersectsCourtyard('U20') && B.intersectsCourtyard('U20')) && !(A.intersectsCourtyard('U22') && B.intersectsCourtyard('U22')) && !(A.intersectsCourtyard('C1') && B.intersectsCourtyard('C1')) && !(A.intersectsCourtyard('C61') && B.intersectsCourtyard('C61')) && !(A.intersectsCourtyard('C62') && B.intersectsCourtyard('C62')) && !(A.intersectsCourtyard('C63') && B.intersectsCourtyard('C63'))")
	(severity error))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Magjack isolation barrier - chip side to line side.
#
# check_creepage CANNOT enforce this: it derives spacing purely from working
# voltage (0.635 mm at 57 V), but this barrier is a hipot / vendor-guidance
# number, not a voltage-derived one. HALO's app note asks 55 mils = 1.40 mm at
# this pitch. See LEARNINGS 2026-07-28 [check_creepage][gates][magnetics].
#
# The J1 land itself was edited at P4 to hold 1.451 mm; this rule stops routing
# from filling the gap that the footprint edit created.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SCOPE CORRECTION (P7). The first draft was
#     A.NetName == 'V48_RAW' && B.intersectsCourtyard('J1')
# which fired on C4 at 1.3666 mm. That was a FALSE POSITIVE, and the reasoning
# matters:
#
# The barrier this rule protects is CABLE-SIDE copper vs PHY-SIDE copper - it
# exists so a surge arriving on the Ethernet cable cannot arc across the
# transformer's land pattern into the PHY. `V48_RAW` is derived from the
# line-side centre taps through the external bridges, so it IS cable-side. Being
# close to J1 is not merely allowed, it is REQUIRED - the bridges have to sit at
# the taps. The old rule was flagging same-domain copper for being where it
# belongs.
#
# What must stay 1.40 mm apart is the 48 V/cable domain from the MDI/PHY domain.
# Note this board is NON-ISOLATED (48 V and logic share one ground), so this is
# surge-withstand geometry, not galvanic isolation - HALO's 55 mil land-pattern
# guidance, which `check_creepage` cannot derive because it is not a function of
# working voltage.
#
# The J1 land itself holds 1.451 mm chip-side to line-side (measured at P4, and
# re-measured at P8). This rule stops ROUTING from closing that gap.
# SECOND SCOPE CORRECTION (P7). The previous draft paired V48_RAW/V48_RTN/+48V_SW
# against the MDI nets - but the magjack's cable-side taps are on
# `/poe/POE_TAP_A1|A2|B1|B2`, and V48_RAW only exists AFTER the external bridges.
# So the rule was watching nets that never come near the barrier, reported zero
# errors, and gave false confidence about a gap that is actually short.
#
# MEASURED barrier (independent geometry, not an assertion):
#   pad 2 (/ETH_TXN) <-> pad 11 (/poe/POE_TAP_A1) = 1.312 mm
#   after shrinking pad 2 from 1.524 -> 1.200 mm (0.150 mm ring on its 0.900 mm
#   drill, exactly JLC's PTH minimum).
#
# HALO's 55 mil (1.40 mm) guidance is NOT met, and cannot be: pads 2 and 11 sit
# one half-pitch apart in x and one pitch in y on a 2.54 mm grid, so even with
# BOTH pads at the annular minimum the ceiling is 2.510 - 1.200 = 1.310 mm. That
# guidance is for HALO's own parts; this vendor's recommended land does not reach
# it at any pad size. Short by 0.088 mm, at the physical maximum.
#
# Residual mitigations: the part's own 2250 VDC hipot barrier (the primary
# isolation, inside the component) and the TPD4E1U06 TVS array on the MDI.
# Board is NON-isolated (48 V and logic share a ground), so this is surge-
# withstand geometry, not a galvanic safety barrier.
#
# Threshold set to 1.30 mm - just under what the land actually achieves - so the
# rule's job is to stop ROUTING from eroding the gap further. Setting it to an
# unreachable 1.40 mm would make it fire permanently and get waived, which is
# worse than a rule that holds a real line.
(rule "magjack_isolation_barrier"
	(constraint clearance (min 1.3mm))
	(condition "(A.NetName == '/poe/POE_TAP_A1' || A.NetName == '/poe/POE_TAP_A2' || A.NetName == '/poe/POE_TAP_B1' || A.NetName == '/poe/POE_TAP_B2') && (B.NetName == '/ETH_TXP' || B.NetName == '/ETH_TXN' || B.NetName == '/ETH_RXP' || B.NetName == '/ETH_RXN') && !(A.Type == 'Pad' && B.Type == 'Pad')")
	(severity error))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PoE tap DIFFERENTIAL pair - A1 vs A2, and B1 vs B2.
#
# check_creepage CANNOT enforce this and never will without a model change.
# It computes dv = v_hv - v_other from constraints.json `voltages`, which is a
# net-to-REFERENCE table, and skips any pair with abs(dv) <= 30 V. All four PoE
# centre taps are declared 57 V, so dv = 0 for these two pairs and they are
# NEVER EVALUATED - yet A1/A2 are the two AC INPUTS of external bridge D2 (and
# B1/B2 of D3), so the full line potential sits across each pair.
#
# This was found at P8 by hand, not by any gate: A1 <-> A2 measured 0.3292 mm
# on all four layers. It was then fixed to 0.6502 mm (F.Cu) / 0.6524 mm (inner,
# B.Cu) by moving both transition vias. A SECOND pair on the same two nets, at
# 0.5500 mm, was hidden behind the first and fixed in the same pass. B1 <-> B2
# was already 1.4778 mm.
#
# This rule exists so DRC holds that line from now on. Without it, any future
# reroute in the D2/D3 fan-in silently reopens a 57 V gap and NOTHING reports it.
# Same role as magjack_isolation_barrier above: encode in DRC what the
# voltage-derived checker structurally cannot express.
#
# Threshold 0.60 mm = IPC-2221B table B2 external, 51-100 V band - deliberately
# NOT the 0.635 mm adopted board-wide, because 0.635 mm is unreachable in the
# north pocket (boxed in by the SHIELD board-lock oval, SHIELD's B.Cu escape and
# the 1.30 mm ETH barrier; the balanced optimum is ~0.65 mm and buying more
# would rob the SHIELD gap to pay this one). A rule set to an unreachable value
# gets waived, which is worse than a rule that holds a real line.
#
# Pad-pair exclusion follows the same reasoning as the HV_48V rules: J1's own
# VC1-VC4 lead frame is package geometry, not a routing choice.
# See LEARNINGS 2026-07-29 [check_creepage][gates].
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
(rule "poe_tap_differential_pair"
	(constraint clearance (min 0.6mm))
	(condition "((A.NetName == '/poe/POE_TAP_A1' && B.NetName == '/poe/POE_TAP_A2') || (A.NetName == '/poe/POE_TAP_B1' && B.NetName == '/poe/POE_TAP_B2')) && !(A.Type == 'Pad' && B.Type == 'Pad')")
	(severity error))
